James Rising provided various estimates of economic loss and damage caused by climate change based on different studies: Markandya & González-Eguino (2018) estimated $179 – 671 billion/year for non-Annex I countries, Songwe et al. (2022) projected $150 – 300 billion/year by 2030, and Newman & Noy (2023) reported market damages of $53 billion/year and VSL-valued losses of $90 billion/year. Rising's own meta-analysis suggests a lower figure of $4 billion/year by 2023. In response, I composed a draft paragraph synthesizing these findings, emphasizing the significant variability in estimates and the importance of developing effective mitigation and adaptation strategies in response to these economic impacts of climate change.
